No, Knuth designed that although Zapf had some fairly significant
input.  That was the placeholder font that Knuth designed to have
something to work with while he was writing TeX.  It's hardly
considered as ugly as you think it is, though that's clearly a matter
of taste. Yeah, the letterforms are a bit spindly, and it's not so
nice on screen, but printed at reasonable resolution it's a
workmanlike font. Places like typophile usually rank it just outside
the top tier of fonts.  I don't know that I've ever seen it used in
professional printing outside of Knuth's books, though--most TeX
documents that I've seen make it to print use some selection of
standard fonts rather than anything TeX-specific.

It's Euler, the other main TeX font, that Zapf was the primary
designer on (Knuth did the Metafont translation but Zapf did all the
design).  That's the one commissioned by the American Mathematical
Society for use in their documents.  It's a very organic Zapf font and
works well as the mathematical display font with other Zapf
creations.  IMO Optima for text body and Euler for formula display is
a beautiful combination.



